#### Article 8

Neglect as referred to in Article 4 letter d includes:  a. acts that result in the failure to fulfill the needs of a child properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or other parties responsible for care;  
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after a child as appropriate, committed by parents, guardians or any other party responsible for their care;  
c. acts that neglect a person within the scope of their household, whereas according to the law applicable to them or due to an agreement or contract they are obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person; and/ord. acts that result in economic dependence by restricting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

#### Article 9

Exploitation as referred to in Article 4 letter e includes:  a. acts that exploit economically or sexually with the intention of benefiting oneself or others;  
b. acts with or without the victim's consent which include but are not limited to prostitution, forced labor or services, slavery or similar practices, oppression, extortion, exploitation of physical, sexual, reproductive organs, or unlawfully removing or transplanting organs and/or body tissues or exploiting the labor or ability of a person by another party to obtain benefits both material and immaterial; and/orc. all forms of exploitation of sexual organs or other body organs of the victim to obtain benefits, including all activities of prostitution or sexual abuse.

# CHAPTER IV

## RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

#### Article 10

Every victim has the right to receive:
a. protection;  
b. information;  
c. optimal services;  
d. continuous handling up to the rehabilitation and reintegration stage; e. confidential handling;  
f. psychological and legal assistance; andg. guarantees of rights related to status as a family member and member of society.

#### Article 11

Child victims of violence, in addition to receiving rights as referred to in Article 10, also receive special rights, as follows:  a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;  
b. the right to basic services;  
c. the right to equal protection;  
d. the right to be free from various stigma; ande. the right to obtain freedom.

# CHAPTER V

## OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

#### Article 12

Obligations and responsibilities in the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence are the joint responsibility of:  a. regional government;  
b. society;  
c. family; andd. parents.

#### Article 13

1. The Regional Government implements efforts to protect women and children victims of violence through:a. implementing policies for the protection of women and children victims of violence adopted by the government;  
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children victims of violence;  
c. establishment and implementation of the functions of P2TP2A;  
d. ensuring the implementation of ease of service to victims;  
e. seeking effectiveness and efficiency for the victim recovery process; andf. seeking the creation of cooperation and coordination in victim recovery efforts.  
2. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, the regional government establishes action programs and activities for the protection of women and children in 1 (one) Regional Action Plan as a basis for regional apparatus in implementing protection for women and children victims of violence.  
3. The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), is further regulated by Mayor Regulation.

#### Article 14

1. The obligations and responsibilities of society as referred to in Article 12 letter b, are implemented in the form of community participation. (2) Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1), include:a. forming family partners at the sub-district level;  
b. forming women and child protection units within community organizations;  
c. conducting independent socialization of women's and children's rights; d. providing first aid to victims; ande. reporting to the competent authority if violence against victims occurs in their environment.  
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and mass media.  
4. Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (2), are implemented responsibly.

#### Article 15

The obligations of family and/or parents as referred to in Article 12 letters c and d, who legally have full responsibility to prevent all forms of violence and protect women and children as family members.

# CHAPTER VI

## IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION

## Part One

#### Article 16

1. The Implementation of Women's Empowerment and Protection of Child Victims of Violence is realized in part through the establishment of integrated and networked UPT formed by the Regional Government, society, non-governmental organizations, donor institutions or other institutions.  
2. UPT as referred to in paragraph (1) is P2TP2A or similar institutions that have the same functions and roles.

## Part Two

### P2TP2A

### Paragraph 1

Establishment

#### Article 17

1. P2TP2A is established in the Region.  
2. The institutional structure of P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) is stipulated by Mayor Decree.  
